    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "KQu4F",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -296,
      "name": "Title",
      "width": 1440,
      "height": 104,
      "content": "AGENTIC OS — BOARD CONCEPTS (wireframe)\nThree takes on the one hard problem (brief §3): keep CLAIMS (what a doc says) visually separate from FACTS (what git says now), and make the DELTA the loudest thing. Convention across all: sans + italic + quotes = a claim (provisional); monospace = a verified machine fact; status = colour + glyph + label. Real data measured 2026-07-26. 6 projects, no scroll @1440×900.\n\nRECOMMENDATION: ship B (Triage) as the default — it wins the 3-second scan and the ambient glance. Borrow C's drift-feed when many docs drift, and use A's claim→delta→fact gutter as the row-hover detail."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "eeQ0k",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 920,
      "name": "Rationale A",
      "width": 1400,
      "height": 150,
      "content": "CONCEPT A — LEDGER   ·   claim-vs-fact as SPATIAL separation.\nEach row reads left→right: CLAIM (sans, italic, aged) → DELTA (the gutter, loudest) → FACT (mono, crisp). The physical centre gutter with hairlines is the ‘gap where they disagree’. Heterogeneous rows solved by equal-height rows, content vertically centred, names/status holding the left scan-line.\n+ Optimises: the epistemic split is unmissable; the delta literally sits between claim and fact.\n− Sacrifices: tall rows = lower density (fills the screen at 6, won't scale past ~8); claim & fact columns compete for width."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "YAuf5",
      "x": 1540,
      "y": 920,
      "name": "Rationale B",
      "width": 1400,
      "height": 150,
      "content": "CONCEPT B — TRIAGE   ·   claim-vs-fact as EDITORIAL correction.  ◀ recommended\nOne dense fixed-height scan-line per project, sorted by severity, with a left severity rail for peripheral/ambient glance. Drift rows expand a second tier: the claim STRUCK THROUGH → the mono correction → a stale badge (copy-editing treatment). Two-tier row solves heterogeneity: scan-line fixed for all; detail only where there is drift; sync rows stay one line.\n+ Optimises: fastest 3-second read + ambient rail; compact; scan-line never breaks.\n− Sacrifices: the drift sentence is squeezed onto one line (truncation risk); fixed columns can crowd long branches."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "BxVly",
      "x": 3080,
      "y": 920,
      "name": "Rationale C",
      "width": 1400,
      "height": 150,
      "content": "CONCEPT C — FEED + ROSTER   ·   drift as a FIRST-CLASS object (answers §9.4 & §9.2).\nTop: a DRIFT FEED of full cards — CLAIMED vs VERIFIED as two labelled, differently-styled blocks (documentary sans vs instrument mono), delta as the headline. Bottom: an ambient ROLE-COVERAGE MATRIX — 7 role columns aligned so the gaps (which drive itqan installs) are scannable at a glance.\n+ Optimises: separates ‘act now’ (cards) from ‘ambient state’ (matrix); roomy claim/fact; role gaps read as a grid.\n− Sacrifices: a project appears twice (feed + roster); the feed grows with drift count, so it scales worst under many simultaneous drifts."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "PEGC5",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 2944,
      "name": "Round 2 Title",
      "width": 1440,
      "height": 150,
      "content": "AGENTIC OS — ROUND 2 (wireframe)\nThe OVERVIEW (forward-looking: what a doc SAYS we're doing) beside the now-built Board (backward: is that doc still TRUE?). Two distinct concepts. Central new job (brief §5.5.1): show PROVENANCE — derived (git) / verified (doc the Board confirms) / suspect (doc with active drift) — without suspect reading as an error. Real data 2026-07-26. Week view = movement + age, never a burndown. Read-only (no +New)."
    },
